@h1 Rendering & styling h1@

One @code .wit code@ source builds to four outputs. The @code -o code@ extension
picks the format; two flags shape the HTML; three levels of styling take you from
zero effort to full art direction.

@h2 Pick a format with -o h2@

The extension on @code -o code@ decides what you get:

@@@pre
wit build doc.wit -o doc.html    ~ complete self-contained styled page
wit build doc.wit -o doc.md      ~ GitHub-flavoured Markdown
wit build doc.wit -o doc.pdf     ~ paginated print-ready PDF
pre@@@

With no @code -o code@, the HTML prints to stdout — handy for piping:

@@@pre
wit build doc.wit | grep '<h1>'
pre@@@

"Self-contained" means the theme's CSS is inlined into the page head: one file
you can open in any browser or email with no stylesheet shipped alongside. An
unrecognised extension is an error, not a guess.

@h2 HTML variants: --fragment and --raw h2@

To drop the content into a page you already have — a blog template, a CMS — ask
for a fragment. You get just the bare @@@code <article> code@@@, no page head and
no theme:

@@@pre
wit build doc.wit --fragment
pre@@@

To keep the full page shell but drop the built-in theme, use @code --raw code@.
It leaves a minimal reset instead, so you bring your own CSS:

@@@pre
wit build doc.wit --raw -o doc.html
pre@@@

@h2 Styling, three levels h2@

@strong Level 1 — the default theme, no work. strong@ Out of the box a built
document looks like a clean word-processor page: a sans-serif body, a comfortable
reading measure, an inch of margin on a soft grey canvas. It follows dark-mode
preference and carries print rules so PDFs paginate sensibly — headings, figures,
and tables kept from breaking across pages. You write no CSS.

@strong Level 2 — small overrides with a style block. strong@ To change one
thing, drop a @@@code @@style … style@@ code@@@ literal into the document. Its CSS
cascades _over_ the theme, touching only what you name. The theme scopes its rules
to the document's own @code .wit-doc code@ class, so scope your overrides there
too:

@@@pre
@@style
.wit-doc { --wit-accent: #2b6a4f; }
style@@
pre@@@

@strong Level 3 — full control with --raw. strong@ Build with @code --raw code@
for a blank canvas, then style everything with your own style blocks and wrapping
nodes. The same CSS drives HTML and PDF, so your print rules carry straight into
the PDF.

Inside a raw @code @@ code@@ node body you can inject a value with
@@@code {{path}} code@@@ interpolation — useful when a colour or size lives in your
data:

@@@pre
@@style
.wit-doc { --wit-accent: {{theme.accent}}; }
style@@
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 @@@code {{path}} code@@@ is live _only_ inside a raw
@code @@ code@@ block's body or string — never in ordinary prose, where it stays
literal. To put a value into running text, define it and reference it by name. aside@

@h2 Markdown, briefly h2@

Markdown output is GitHub-flavoured: headings, bold and italic, lists, pipe
tables, blockquotes. Right for a README, an issue, or any portable plain text.
A few HTML-only features degrade — a hard line break becomes a space, and embedded
audio or video has no Markdown equivalent.

@h2 PDF needs a browser h2@

PDF paginates the HTML through a headless Chrome, so a system Chrome or Chromium
must be installed (or point @code WIT_CHROME code@ at a browser executable).
Without one, the build stops and names the fix. HTML and Markdown need no browser.

